{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games","id":1006024,"title":"Open Table model with CoD games","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-20T23:40:36-08:00","id":1006024,"title":"Open Table model with CoD games","content":"Having lately some players schedule issues in normal chronicles, I start to think how to resolve the problems of \u2018we cannot gather by three months in a row\u2019. One interesting solution is Open Table idea \u2013 game model in that particular characters are not necessary for the play. Most classical examples is D&amp;D megadungeon, with nearest town in that new explorers can \u2018spawn\u2019 instantly and join party on each session of campaign.<br /> <br /> More on Open Table idea you can read here - <a href=\"http://thealexandrian.net/wordpress/1223/roleplaying-games/opening-your-game-table\" target=\"_blank\">http://thealexandrian.net/wordpress/...our-game-table</a><br /> <br /> Chronicles of Darkness games are made much more characters-centered than D&amp;D, still, so I have somehow problem in envision this model of play with them. Have you got some patents or advices on who to port it for new chronicles?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T00:47:36-08:00","id":1006044,"title":"","content":"The Open Table concept doesn't really mesh well with the default playstyle the CofD encourages.<br /> <br /> There are some game-lines that can handle it. Mummy is probably the best. Have shorter games mostly oriented around small things where the PCs are woken up by their cults. Can't make it? Your cult didn't wake you up this time. With everyone sleeping away and all that, this sort of thing can work. Save the big Sothic Cycle stuff for when the group can gather in full for more time.<br /> <br /> Beasts, Demons, and Prometheans make decent &quot;back up&quot; characters that can drop into other game focused stuff pretty easily. So if you set up the more &quot;group&quot; oriented games like Changeling, Werewolf, and Mage so that the &quot;core&quot; group you need to play is 1/3 of the total people involved, and that tends to dictate what you're playing, set them all in nearby locations so someone can just have their Beast drop in and get involved in whatever the Uratha are up to on gaming session, and so on. If you're doing the one metro-area thing, you can also have a backup of collaborative world building play instead of traditional gaming. Rather than foist everything on one participant, you can have some nights where you do things like build the local Freehold as a group with people making stat-less historical figures to give history to it as it gets flushed out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T01:08:34-08:00","id":1006048,"title":"","content":"For more political themed games (Vampire, Mage, Changeling, Hunter and Demon come to mind but you could probably do others) you could try a slightly more LARP-ish or PBP Sandbox approach. Lots of PCs each with their own goals and subplots running in eachother's background. The players are less a regular party but just whoever happens to be at a particular location when some plot-thing happens.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9065-darkblade","name":"Darkblade","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"78"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T01:12:08-08:00","id":1006050,"title":"","content":"I think about running mix of historical ( Viking ) and modern <b>Werewolf</b> based on the same Protectorate members, across centuries, much less correct Pack centered. Or PCs assuming various characters from the extensive Pack that has about 20-30 members.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T11:24:49-08:00","id":1006302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1006044#post1006044\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Open Table concept doesn't really mesh well with the default playstyle the CofD encourages.</div> </div> </div> </div>I couldn't disagree more. CofD works <i>great</i> for &quot;open table&quot;, that's part of why it works so well for LARP and Play-by-post.<br /> <br /> You just have to change the way you design your stories. Instead of assuming a Coterie, a Pack, a Cabal, a Motley, etc. working together to accomplish a common goal, assume individuals with goals that may sometimes coincide and may sometimes clash. Allow temporary alliances and emnities to form and dissolve organically. Allow players to engage in PvP. Use individual Beats instead of group Beats. Allow the action to be primary player-driven. Then design scenes that will advance the plots as events that will happen, with or without the characters present. Almost like a backdrop to the main event of the player-driven action. Make sure the outcomes of these scenes have long-term consequences. Make more scenes in more different parts of the city than the characters can be present for, so the city feels alive and operating independently of the player characters. Its a challenging style of play to run, but it works <i>great</i> in CofD if you can pull it off.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T16:51:36-08:00","id":1006466,"title":"","content":"There's a section on something similar in the <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/3510/Vampire-Players-Guide--Revised-Edition\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Masquerade Revised Players Guide</a> for planning troupe-style games, from players creating a few characters and sharing some of them up to nobody having their own character and rotating Storyteller duty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Craig Oxbrow<br /> <b>The Trinity Continuum</b> freelancer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/221-craig-oxbrow","name":"Craig Oxbrow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar221_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-21T23:34:41-08:00","id":1006608,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1006302#post1006302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I couldn't disagree more. CofD works <i>great</i> for &quot;open table&quot;, that's part of why it works so well for LARP and Play-by-post.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like you're making some assumptions of what &quot;open table&quot; is supposed to mean. It's not just breaking down the traditional adventure party for a group of characters that aren't so bound to each other that it feels like you have to have everyone present every time you play. It's also supposed to include things like easily adding new characters so things don't get lopsided in terms of what the group can do (though that's a bit less of a problem for most CofD games), and a number of other things.<br /> <br /> I'm also not thinking that you're really disagreeing with me here. I said &quot;default&quot; playstyle, and a list of all the ways you can do different playstyles just sort of reinforces the idea that the assumed default doesn't work well; and thus needs change.<br /> <br /> Though this also means needing to be really careful about how to approach &quot;group&quot; stuff. A lot of the games have some sort of shared resource that ties PC groups together, and is probably where there's a need for some mechanical adjustments over just playstyle ones.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-22T13:14:49-08:00","id":1006829,"title":"","content":"I've not yet successfully attempted it, but running an open table game has been on my list for a while. The set up that I have most material for (and which I think might make the best fit for the format overall) is a network of Hunter cells coordinating activities in a major city.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-22T17:45:03-08:00","id":1006926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1006829#post1006829\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've not yet successfully attempted it, but running an open table game has been on my list for a while. The set up that I have most material for (and which I think might make the best fit for the format overall) is a network of Hunter cells coordinating activities in a major city.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can probably handle a Werewolf game with a Protectorate that hands out hunts in this fashion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-22T21:11:20-08:00","id":1007019,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1006926#post1006926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Can probably handle a Werewolf game with a Protectorate that hands out hunts in this fashion.</div> </div> </div> </div>Certainly. Could probably handle a Changeling or Vampire court as well, but at that point we might as well just stand up from the table and call it a LARP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-22T23:42:33-08:00","id":1007062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1006926#post1006926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Can probably handle a Werewolf game with a Protectorate that hands out hunts in this fashion.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1007019#post1007019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Certainly. Could probably handle a Changeling or Vampire court as well, but at that point we might as well just stand up from the table and call it a LARP.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hunting is very different from politicking, in such that consequences and motivations of characters are secondary to it. In politics continuity ( i.e. same characters actions ) is must have it. In hunting, each Prey can be taken by different group, in reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-23T01:37:15-08:00","id":1007098,"title":"","content":"Why're you quoting me up there?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T13:04:15-08:00","id":1019938,"title":"","content":"This is more about <a href=\"http://thealexandrian.net/wordpress/38659/roleplaying-games/open-table-manifesto-part-2-what-an-open-table-needs\" target=\"_blank\">Open Table requirements</a> - that I think are impossible to meet in <b>CoD</b> games. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Maybe coming only on some approximation to this model?<br /> <br /> I still wonder how to bite a thing with my imagined <b>Werewolf</b> game about modern and past Scandinavia. I was thinking about core players hailing from one Pack and added player becoming Wolf-Blooded and humans from Pack. But then I think - 'How this will work? One session I have 4 main PCs, next I have 1? And what about jumping between timelines?' My head hurt on thinking about it all. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T15:13:19-08:00","id":1019997,"title":"","content":"Eh. What's the difference between this Open Table Model and making every session a one-shot, exactly?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T18:29:49-08:00","id":1020099,"title":"","content":"Open Table assumes continuity between gaming sessions; at least for the setting. If the town is under threat of a raiding bandits and the group that was playing last week took out the bandits, the group this week comes into the game with those bandits out of the picture and something new to deal with/some consequence from that.<br /> <br /> As well, Open Table style assumes players will generally keep their character/main character to use most of the time and that character will gain XP (or whatever) due to use instead of starting over with a new character every time. Having back up characters is mostly to make sure that whatever make up you have that day is relatively balanced for the demands of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T17:28:30-08:00","id":1020722,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty surprised that everyone is arguing the open game model lends itself to D&amp;D but not CofD. From a purely game-balance standpoint I find that it's the opposite: in D&amp;D you really need a certain number of players or you can't win a fight, and if you don't have your healer, skill monkey, damage dealer, etc. you can't get through a dungeon. Whereas, since CofD is more narrative, you can have people drop in and out and change characters freely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12761-suzuki","name":"Suzuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12761_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T18:10:38-08:00","id":1020738,"title":"","content":"But the strength of the Open Table model is that it assumes players will have multiple characters (even making new ones that day) to ensure a stable of PCs to cover the essential party roles for a game like D&amp;D. D&amp;D is also really a stand in for any procedural focused game (treating dungeon crawling as the narrative equivalent to a procedural in the more traditional sense). Shadowrun or Leverage are also easy to apply the Open Table concept to (though Shadowrun can be hard if you're letting people make new characters during gaming time). Having major role-niches is important, but again that's why most players are assumed to have access to multiple characters so the party can be arranged as needed.<br /> <br /> The issue with games like the CofD is the games focus on introspective narratives over time. Bob the werewolf kills his wolf-blooded brother during a bout of Kuruth, and then Bob's player misses three sessions and misses out on the resolution of that dramatic plot point. Bob comes back into the story still in, &quot;I just killed my brother!&quot; space, but the story itself has moved on without him. This isn't an unsolvable issue. There are lots of ways to address it, but it's where the concern with the default CofD play style comes in. The focus on introspective narrative over procedural narrative makes breaks in cast a problem that needs solved.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T09:11:03-08:00","id":1020914,"title":"","content":"I've been running an open table D&amp;D game based on those blog posts for a while now, and trying to think about how to make CofD work for it. A big part is the basic structure of the campaign needs to change. Less focus on personal, introspective horror, character development, and overarching plots. More focus on character interaction and accomplishing personal objectives. I think it would be very hard to port an existing campaign to an open table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2021-magicswordsman","name":"MagicSwordsman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"194"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T14:34:37-08:00","id":1021010,"title":"","content":"I think I might be a bit confused at what Open Table is, because I still see no reason why CofD wouldn't work for that.<br /> <br /> Inserting new character is far easier to do than in D&amp;D because almost all members of a Splat's Society is either that splat or humans. And all are viable character options, unlike D&amp;D where adventurers are suppose to be exceptional individuals compared to most NPCs.<br /> <br /> Different splat have far more reasons to work together in CofD than most races do in D&amp;D because racial enmity is far more situation and region specific in CofD than D&amp;D. (Case and point try getting an Elf and an Orc to work together without breaking traditional cannon.)<br /> <br /> And in instances where personal horror comes into play there is nothing stopping the ST from taking that player aside and just doing a solo game to illustrate consequences, or even just working out a narrative without needing any rolls. That way the flow would hold and the characters would change and grow organically without removing the personal horror.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T14:34:37-08:00","id":1021011,"title":"","content":"[Accidental double post]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Not sure how, but the above posted twice. 12-22-2016, 05:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T17:37:44-08:00","id":1021086,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dusksage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1006024-open-table-model-with-cod-games?p=1021010#post1021010\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I might be a bit confused at what Open Table is, because I still see no reason why CofD wouldn't work for that.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not that you can do CoD using Open Table methodology, but that the approach changes the focus of the game when you do. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2021-magicswordsman\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2021\" data-vbnamecard=\"2021\">MagicSwordsman</a> pointed out, it doesn't work as well for games that focus on personal or introspective horror.<br /> <br /> One of the big reasons for this is the whole point of Open Table is to counter the, &quot;we're older and everyone getting together at the same time to play is impossible&quot; effect, The idea is to have a time when you run the game, and whoever shows shows, and you play with what you have. That's why there are techniques that go along with it like having multiple pre-made characters (both player based to make sure they have a character that plays well with the other characters there that day, and from the GM for new players to the game/group to have something to go with ASAP).<br /> <br /> One of the big problems with CoD and Open Table, is that Open Table relies heavily on (not to be dismissive) interchangeable characters. For Open Table to work, I need to be able to say, &quot;Oh, Sarah couldn't come today, so instead of my normal PC, I'm going to break out my social character to make up for hers not being in the group.&quot; In the CoD that assumption goes against a lot of how the games tend to treat player groups. A werewolves operate in packs that are too insular for that sort of swapping in and out of characters with the same specialty/niche/role. That's why D&amp;D generally works for it. You can always set up the game as adventurers all part of the same guild that form impromptu teams based on the current jobs/expeditions/etc. available (aka the DM sets up stuff for the group that's there). The idea that one member isn't around and someone else is filling in isn't a big deal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And in instances where personal horror comes into play there is nothing stopping the ST from taking that player aside and just doing a solo game to illustrate consequences, or even just working out a narrative without needing any rolls. That way the flow would hold and the characters would change and grow organically without removing the personal horror. </div> </div> </div>But, this is the sort of thing Open Table wants to avoid. The whole goal is to ensure that things don't get bogged down by non-game time issues. If you need to have special side session with individual players, you're getting bogged down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T18:34:18-08:00","id":1021101,"title":"","content":"Ah, ok. I see what you're saying now. And you do make some excellent points. Especially in regards too the ease of play vs. personal horror elements. I can see how the two would greatly hinder each other in this system, and that CofD's core themes are too closely tied to personal horror. <br /> <br /> It may be possible to change some of the focus from personal horror to a theme more common in more Open Table friendly games using some of the setting hacks from Mirrors, but as is the Horror setting for CofD does present too many complications to the formula.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T01:44:36-08:00","id":1021192,"title":"","content":"I've had an idea that I've been noodling on: a kind of stupidly-massive epic where you use the Microscope RPG to define a sweep of history before getting into real CofD play. One thing I've been considering is when to dispense with history-making and to dive in and actually create full-blown characters with XP and stuff. Is it the first time we play a Scene, or should we go a few first? Do we try to hit an entire Period, with some foreknowledge of That Which Will Occur Later (suddenly, prophecies are easy!)? I'm not sure what would work and I haven't had a chance to get people to try it.<br /> <br /> And it occurs to me that this can be adapted somewhat to Open Table CofD in that, for any given span of history, or even any given story, you just map it to some combination of players. Bob goes missing for three weeks? Fine. We'll play with what was happening fifty years earlier. New player? Well, let's mess with what's going on a decade later. The problem with this strategy is that it's probably not very fulfilling: if some combination of players never meets again, that story will never wrap up. But... that happens anyways and we figure out how to deal, so I don't think it's a huge problem. It's also probably very daunting for a new player, but as long as everyone is on board and willing to cooperate and figure out how to ease them into the mix gently, that might work.<br /> <br /> What's fun about it to me is that it really messes with time. You might be fighting a Big Bad, but when you go fifty years previous, that might be a PC and he might make decisions about his character's motivations and ambitions that suddenly inform the present time of a diplomatic opportunity (yeeees, my character happened to find his journal! totally by chance), which might mean an apocalyptic event the group playing the decade-later timeline thought was caused by the Big Bad was actually just pinned on him, and there's a Bigger Bad somewhere out there they haven't discovered yet, or maybe it's a winners-write-history power play illustrating this <i>other</i> theme.<br /> <br /> It might not work well for immortality shticks, but even Mummy would probably handle it fine because you'll have different combinations of insomniacs. (I don't actually know anything about Mummy.) It's certainly not as flexible as an open table of D&amp;D megadungeoning, but well... what do you guys think?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T01:46:13-08:00","id":1021193,"title":"","content":"I still thinks that Pack-as-Troupe mechanics in <b>Werewolf</b> could works - it's just it makes really difficult for 'main plot' idea for each chronicle. On first game session I can have four Uratha core of Pack. Then on next one I go with two Uratha 'main players' take and use two Wolf-Blooded for gamers that come this time around. Problem starts when for each session you have completely different party members. I'm not even saying about how ridicules can 'character swaping' be between sessions. Problem is even with coherent plot.<br /> <br /> My imagined for now Open Table chronicle would run like this:<br /> <b>Session 1: </b>Main 4 Uratha of the Pack going after main chronicle antagonist.<br /> <b>Session 2: </b>We end with 2 Uratha ( what get to other 2 werewolves they not show up? ) and we get 2 next Wolf-Blooded helping with next Hunt?<br /> <b>Session 3: </b>We end with 1 Uratha of core Pack and we have 2 Wolf-Blooded and 1 human of the Pack - So I should continue plot of Session 1, Session 2, or maybe do prequel or sidequel? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <b>Repeated few next iterations:</b> &lt;Total plot chaos!&gt;<br /> <br /> Not to mentioned I wanted to made parallel Viking plot sessions... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 12-23-2016, 04:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}